  "selection": {
    "seed": 20260925,
    "criteria": {
      "changed_files": "the cleaned fix changes exactly one file, a JavaScript source file (not a test)",
      "max_hunks": 3,
      "max_changed_head_lines": 20,
      "recorded_tests": "where the dataset records test results: at least one test fails with the fix's tests added but not on the buggy revision (a bug whose recorded results show none is excluded); where it records none, the bug is kept and its evidence is BugsJS's manual validation only",
      "diff_agreement": "git's diff and the harness's difflib diff give the same head-side hunks",
      "text": "UTF-8, no carriage returns"
    },
    "method": "per project: every bug meeting the criteria, then a seeded sample"
  },
  "threats": [
    "Reversal is not a natural pull request: the 'change' is a fix undone, so its shape (size, what surrounds it) differs from how bugs are really introduced.",
    "Contamination: Express, Karma, Hexo and ESLint are popular public projects, and BugsJS is public; a language model may have seen both the code and the fixes.",
    "Selection: only small, single-file, JavaScript-source fixes (see criteria), which favors localized bugs.",
    "Executable evidence is BugsJS's RECORDED test run, not a run by this project (no project test suite was executed here). For Karma and ESLint the dataset snapshot records no test results, so those cases rest on BugsJS's manual validation alone; each case says which (see test_evidence).",
    "Ground truth is where the fix changed code; a finding elsewhere that also points at the bug is scored as unmatched."
  ],
